4 Experimental Setup
4.1 Datasets and Tasks
We use two complementary sources for joint post-training. Medical trajectory synthesis. For the OpenAI Health Bench [29] Consensus subset, each sample consists of a clinical instruction and a set of rubrics (scoring criteria covering factual accuracy, completeness, reasoning depth, safety, etc.). We synthesize response trajectories by providing GPT-5.1 with both the instruction and the full set of rubrics for each sample, prompting it to generate a response that satisfies the rubric dimensions jointly. Biological knowledge synthesis. We use the NCBI-derived biological dataset from GenePT [26]: biomedical corpus from NCBI is used as the knowledge source in EasyDataset [30], and GPT-OSS-120B [31] synthesizes instruction-response pairs covering broad biological knowledge.
For Medical Competence , we evaluate LLMs on the OpenAI Health Bench [29] Hard subset, reporting both theme-wise and axis-wise results. For Biological Process Reasoning , we use GeneAgent benchmarks [9] and replace the GPT-4o backbone with BFT-aligned LLMs while keeping the agent pipeline unchanged. For Chemical Perturbation Reasoning , we evaluate on VCWorld [10], where models predict whether a target gene is differentially expressed after drug treatment across five cancer cell lines; BFT-aligned LLMs replace the Gemini-2.5-Flash backbone under the same pipeline. To assess RL compatibility, we further train SFT/DFT/BFT-aligned LLMs with GRPO on 45 Tahoe-100M cell lines [32] using sparse binary rewards and evaluate on the five held-out VCWorld cell lines. For General Capabilities , we report 5-shot MMLU [33] and CMMLU [34]. For Representation Tasks , we evaluate text-derived embeddings on gene-level prediction, cell-level clustering and multimodal integration, and perturbation response prediction with STATE datasets [35].
