4 Experimental Setup
4.2 Models and Baselines
We employ the DeepSeek-R1-Distill series (14B Qwen, 32B Qwen, and 70B Llama) [1] as our base models. These models possess general reasoning capabilities, making them suitable for testing biomedical alignment. All three model sizes are jointly trained on the combined medical and biological datasets described above. We compare BFT against the following baselines: SFT : the standard negative log-likelihood objective. DFT [5]: a dynamic re-weighting method that scales loss based on token-level confidence to stabilize gradients. BFT-token or BFT-sample : ablation that uses only token-level or sample-level reweighting. Agent Systems with Backbone Replacement : for biological process reasoning, we use the GeneAgent [9] framework and replace its default GPT-4o backbone with SFT/DFT/BFT-aligned 70B models; for chemical perturbation reasoning, we use the VCWorld [10] framework and replace its Gemini-2.5-Flash backbone. For representation tasks, we compare against scGPT [23], GenePT [26], and STATE [35] as domain-specific baselines. All models are fine-tuned using the LlamaFactory framework [36], following the standard supervised fine-tuning recipe provided by the framework. We keep the optimization recipe identical, and all training and inference experiments are conducted on NVIDIA H20 GPUs.
Figure 3: General capability retention, robustness and safety. a : MMLUand CMMLU evaluations. b : BFT performance under different window sizes g . c : Post-training safety test.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel experimental results figure with bar charts and line plots comparing model variants. Panel a shows MMLU and CMMLU accuracy for 14B, 32B, and 70B models across categories including Average, STEM, Social Science, Humanities, and Other. The legend compares Base, DFT, BFT-token, BFT, SFT, and BFT-sample variants. Panel b shows line plots of score or accuracy versus window size for Medicine, GeneAgent, VCWorld, and MMLU, with shaded error bands. Panel c shows a horizontal bar chart of win-preference rate (%) comparing Base, BFT, BFT-token, DFT, BFT-sample, and SFT, where SFT has the highest visible preference rate and Base the lowest.

--- CHUNK 16 ---
4 Experimental Setup
4.2 Models and Baselines
Figure 4: Unified representation across genetic and cellular levels. a : UMAP visualization of gene embeddings. b : Gene-level downstream performance on biological property prediction and gene interaction prediction. c : Cell-level representation performance on phenotype and cell-type.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel scientific figure comparing gene embedding/model methods.

Panel a: Three 2D scatter/UMAP-style plots labeled `scGPT`, `GenePT`, and `BFT`, showing gene categories as colored points. Legend includes gene biotypes such as protein_coding, lncRNA, miRNA, rRNA, pseudogene, IG_V_gene, TR_V_gene, snoRNA, snRNA, and others. The visible finding is that GenePT and BFT show more separated clusters by gene biotype than scGPT, where points appear more mixed.

Panel b: Bar charts of AUC performance for methods `scGPT`, `GenePT`, and `BFT`. Left chart is `Single gene input` with x-axis tasks including `TF range`, `Dosage sensitivity`, `Biased in male`, `Biased in female`, and `Bivalent in hPAs`. Right chart is `Multi gene input` with tasks `GDA` and `PPI`. BFT generally has the highest or near-highest AUC across several tasks.

Panel c: Bar charts of clustering performance. Left chart is `Clustering performance (phenotype)` with metrics `ARI`, `AMI`, and `ASW`; right chart is `Clustering performance (cell type)` with the same metrics. Methods compared are `PCA`, `scGPT`, `GenePT`, and `BFT`. GenePT/BFT appear stronger than PCA/scGPT on several clustering metrics, especially AMI for cell type.
Figure 5: Comparison of single-cell perturbation response prediction results.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel bar plot comparing model performance across four cell lines: `hepg2`, `rpe1`, `jurkat`, and `k562`. Each panel has y-axis `Score` and x-axis categories `DES`, `PDS`, and `MAE`. Bars are grouped by legend entries `BFT`, `STATE-SE`, and `STATE-small`, with error bars shown.

Main visible finding: `PDS` scores are highest across all panels, generally around 0.6-0.7 with large error bars. `DES` scores are lower, varying by cell line and model, while `MAE` scores are near zero across all cell lines.
Table 2: Comparison of multimodal integration on the scIB benchmark [37]. Bio conservation (Isolated labels, KMeans NMI, cLISI) and Batch correction (Silhouette batch, iLISI, Graph connectivity) are reported per-metric; the Aggregate columns follow the scIB-defined min-max-normalized weighted aggregation (not the arithmetic mean of the per-metric columns), and Total = 0 . 4 Batch + 0 . 6 Bio.
