4 Experimental Setup
4.2 Models and Baselines
We employ the DeepSeek-R1-Distill series (14B Qwen, 32B Qwen, and 70B Llama) [1] as our base models. These models possess general reasoning capabilities, making them suitable for testing biomedical alignment. All three model sizes are jointly trained on the combined medical and biological datasets described above. We compare BFT against the following baselines: SFT : the standard negative log-likelihood objective. DFT [5]: a dynamic re-weighting method that scales loss based on token-level confidence to stabilize gradients. BFT-token or BFT-sample : ablation that uses only token-level or sample-level reweighting. Agent Systems with Backbone Replacement : for biological process reasoning, we use the GeneAgent [9] framework and replace its default GPT-4o backbone with SFT/DFT/BFT-aligned 70B models; for chemical perturbation reasoning, we use the VCWorld [10] framework and replace its Gemini-2.5-Flash backbone. For representation tasks, we compare against scGPT [23], GenePT [26], and STATE [35] as domain-specific baselines. All models are fine-tuned using the LlamaFactory framework [36], following the standard supervised fine-tuning recipe provided by the framework. We keep the optimization recipe identical, and all training and inference experiments are conducted on NVIDIA H20 GPUs.
Figure 3: General capability retention, robustness and safety. a : MMLUand CMMLU evaluations. b : BFT performance under different window sizes g . c : Post-training safety test.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Multi-panel experimental results figure with bar charts and line plots comparing model variants. Panel a shows MMLU and CMMLU accuracy for 14B, 32B, and 70B models across categories including Average, STEM, Social Science, Humanities, and Other. The legend compares Base, DFT, BFT-token, BFT, SFT, and BFT-sample variants. Panel b shows line plots of score or accuracy versus window size for Medicine, GeneAgent, VCWorld, and MMLU, with shaded error bands. Panel c shows a horizontal bar chart of win-preference rate (%) comparing Base, BFT, BFT-token, DFT, BFT-sample, and SFT, where SFT has the highest visible preference rate and Base the lowest.

